WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.405
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.394 --> 00:00:09.060
SARA ROBINSON: My
name is Sara Robinson.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.170
I'm a developer advocate on
the Google Cloud Platform team.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.264
You can find me on
Twitter at srobtweets.

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.430
And I'm going to be joined
later in the presentation

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:17.930
by Nick Hasty,
from our customer,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:20.750
GIPHY, and my teammate,
Zach Akil, another developer

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.250
advocate.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:25.930
So let's get started by talking
about what is machine learning?

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.530
So at a high level,
machine learning

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.380
involves teaching computers
to recognize patterns

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.509
in the same way
that our brains do.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:34.250
So as humans, it's
really easy for us

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.920
to distinguish between
a cat and a dog.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.440
But it's much more difficult
to teach a computer

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.150
to be able to do the same thing.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.107
But how do we actually get
from input to prediction?

00:00:45.107 --> 00:00:47.690
This is going to depend on the
type of problem you're solving.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.712
I like to think of
it along a spectrum.

00:00:49.712 --> 00:00:51.170
So on the left-hand
side, let's say

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.378
you're solving a generic
task, something that someone

00:00:53.378 --> 00:00:55.010
has already solved before.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.809
On the opposite side
of the spectrum,

00:00:56.809 --> 00:00:58.850
maybe you're solving
something very custom that's

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.629
specific to your data set.

00:01:01.629 --> 00:01:03.920
Let's think of this more
specifically in terms of image

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.030
classification.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:07.700
So for example, let's say
we have this image of a cat.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.220
So the input to our model
is the pixels in the image,

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.970
and we want our model
to output the label,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.346
that there's a
cat in this image.

00:01:13.346 --> 00:01:15.845
So many of you know this is a
pretty common machine learning

00:01:15.845 --> 00:01:16.497
task.

00:01:16.497 --> 00:01:18.830
There's lots of pre-trained
models out there that exist,

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.440
that have been trained
to identify a broad range

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.844
of categories and images.

00:01:22.844 --> 00:01:24.510
So you don't need to
start from scratch.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.630
You can utilize
an existing model.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.240
But let's say we
have the same image

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.220
and we want-- this
cat's name is Chloe.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.010
And we want our model to be
able to identify Chloe apart

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.057
from the other cats
in our image library.

00:01:36.057 --> 00:01:37.640
And maybe we even
want our model to be

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.220
able to draw a bounding box
around her in the image.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.920
So this is going to require
us to provide our own training

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.260
data so that our model
is able to differentiate

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.416
between the different
cats in our image library.

00:01:48.416 --> 00:01:49.790
Let's think of
this one other way

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:53.390
in terms of natural
language processing.

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:56.467
So let's take, for example,
this text from one of my tweets.

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:59.050
Let's say I want to extract the
parts of speech from the text.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.960
I want my model to be
able to tell me what words

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.720
are verbs, nouns, and pronouns.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.385
This is a pretty common natural
language processing task.

00:02:07.385 --> 00:02:09.259
So again, I don't need
to start from scratch.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:11.770
I can utilize an existing
pre-trained model

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:13.420
to help me with that.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.120
But let's say I've
got the same input

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.796
and I want my model
to know that this

00:02:17.796 --> 00:02:20.170
is a tweet about programming,
and more specifically, it's

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:21.697
a tweet about Google Cloud.

00:02:21.697 --> 00:02:23.280
So for that to work,
I'm going to need

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.960
to give my model hundreds
or thousands of tweets

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.350
about each of these topics.

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.680
So that it's going to be able
to generalize and extract

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.872
those topics from new tweets
that it hasn't seen before.

00:02:34.872 --> 00:02:36.330
So in this talk,
I want to show you

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.210
how easy it is to add machine
learning to your application

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:41.530
using many tools we have
available on Google Cloud

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.900
Platform.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.352
So starting on the
left-hand side,

00:02:44.352 --> 00:02:45.810
are products that
are targeted more

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.370
towards application developers.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.310
We have a set of
machine-learning APIs.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.380
So these APIs give you
access to pre-trained models.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.522
You just need to write
one REST API request.

00:02:55.522 --> 00:02:57.480
And you can access all
these pre-trained models

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:58.590
that we've already
trained for you.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.860
You don't need to provide
any of your training data.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:03.060
So it requires no machine
learning expertise to get

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.290
started.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.754
On the other side
of the spectrum,

00:03:05.754 --> 00:03:07.920
we have products that are
targeted more towards data

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.128
scientists and machine-learning
practitioners, people

00:03:10.128 --> 00:03:12.910
that have a little bit
more ML experience.

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.480
So for this, we have Cloud
Machine Learning Engine,

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:18.110
which is our manage platform
for training and serving

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:20.640
your own custom
machine-learning models.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.530
So until recently,
you'll notice there was

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.240
kind of a gap in the middle.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.790
This is why I'm super
excited about AutoML,

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.950
which you may have heard
about in the keynote.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.040
So AutoML is a tool that lets
you train your own custom

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.280
machine-learning models
without writing model code.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.830
So it lets you get a little
bit more custom than you would

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.799
with the APIs, for example.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:41.340
So you provide your
own training data

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.090
currently available for
images, texts, and translation.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.340
So now that you know the
three different product areas

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.350
we're going to be
covering, let's dive right

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:52.800
in with machine
learning as an API.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.734
So as I mentioned, we
have five pre-trained APIs

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:57.900
that give you access to
pre-trained machine-learning

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.080
models to accomplish
common ML tasks,

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.740
like analyzing images,
analyzing scenes in a video,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.740
converting audio to
text, analyzing that text

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.870
a little further, and
then finally translating

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.740
that text into over 100
different languages.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.340
I'm going to focus on just
two of the APIs, vision

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:18.070
and natural language, to
give you a taste of what

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:21.180
the APIs can provide.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.850
So starting within vision,
these are all the features

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.057
of our Cloud Vision API.

00:04:25.057 --> 00:04:27.390
So at its core, the Vision
API will answer the question,

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:28.510
what is this a picture of?

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.000
That's label detection.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.770
So for this image, it might
return elephant, animal,

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.340
et cetera.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.050
Web detection will
go a step further,

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.960
and it will look across the
web for similar, or identical,

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:39.850
images.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:41.600
And based on the
content of those pages,

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.790
it will return tags back for
you on what's in your picture.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.040
OCR stands for optical
character recognition.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.230
So this can extract
text from images,

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:53.050
it can tell you
where that text is,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.410
and it can even understand
the language of that text.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:58.420
Skipping ahead to explicit
content detection.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:01.212
This will tell you is my
image appropriate or not.

00:05:01.212 --> 00:05:02.920
This applies to pretty
much any site that

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.730
has user-generated content.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:07.450
So instead of having someone
manually screen each image,

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:09.670
you just make one API
call that'll tell you,

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:12.860
is this image
appropriate or not?

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:14.895
So this is what a Vision
API request looks like.

00:05:14.895 --> 00:05:16.270
You'll notice
here, we're passing

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:19.750
in the URL of an image
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.600
You can also pass in
your base64 encoded image

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.335
directly in your API requests.

00:05:24.335 --> 00:05:25.960
And then in the
features block, we just

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.690
tell the API what types of
detection we want to run.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:32.390
So it's just a rest API.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:33.840
You can call it in any language.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.660
I've chosen to include
a Python example here.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:39.540
So we have many client libraries
to help you access these APIs

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.030
in your favorite languages.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.670
So here we're using the Google
Cloud Vision module for Python.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:47.617
We're creating an
image annotator client.

00:05:47.617 --> 00:05:49.200
And then we're running
label detection

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:52.760
and analyzing those responses.

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:55.421
So I never like to get too far
into a talk without a demo.

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:56.420
So let's go to the demo.

00:06:00.266 --> 00:06:02.390
So to show you what's
possible with the Vision API,

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:04.210
this is the Vision
API product page.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:07.840
And you can try out all of our
APIs directly in the browser

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.620
by uploading your own images.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:12.340
So here, what I can do
is I can upload an image.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.230
I'm going to choose
this selfie that I

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:18.910
took when I was seeing Hamilton
a couple of months ago.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:22.066
And we can see everything we
get back from the Vision API.

00:06:22.066 --> 00:06:23.440
So face detection
is able to tell

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.730
where my face is in the image.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:27.400
It's also able to
detect emotion.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.960
So it says, joy is very likely.

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:32.170
I was super excited to be seeing
Hamilton, so that is accurate.

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:34.450
It also identifies
where different features

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.190
are in my face.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.470
If we skip ahead
to web detection,

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.420
based on other pictures
of the same theater,

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:42.460
it's able to identify
the exact theater where

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:44.626
I'm standing even though
the lighting in the picture

00:06:44.626 --> 00:06:46.090
isn't great.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.100
My favorite part
about this is what's

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:50.380
returned from our
text-detection end point.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:51.880
So when I first
uploaded this image,

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:53.213
I ran it through the Vision API.

00:06:53.213 --> 00:06:55.400
I didn't even see any
text in the image.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.400
But you'll notice that
the word Playbill is here

00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.560
on my program book,
and the API is

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.560
able to identify that for us.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.940
And then skipping ahead here,
we can look at the full JSON

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:08.450
response right in the browser.

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.950
So I encourage you all to try
this out with your own images.

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:12.533
See if the Vision
API is right for you

00:07:12.533 --> 00:07:14.680
before you start
writing any code.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:15.870
So that's the Vision API.

00:07:15.870 --> 00:07:18.300
We can go back to the slides
and I'll include a link

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.970
to that demo at the end.

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:22.770
So that was the Vision API.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:26.200
Moving right along into
Cloud Natural language.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.050
So the Natural
Language API provides

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.620
four different features.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.767
First, it lets you extract
entities from text.

00:07:32.767 --> 00:07:34.350
And then it lets you
detect sentiment.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.200
So it can tell you, is this
text positive or negative.

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.700
If you want to get
a little bit more

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.590
into the linguistic
details of your text,

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:43.420
you can use the Analyze
syntax end point.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:45.270
And then finally, the
newest feature of this

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:46.860
is the content classification.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.010
So you can classify
your text into over 700

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.817
different categories.

00:07:52.817 --> 00:07:54.900
This is an example of
calling the Natural Language

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.190
API in Node.js.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.320
Again, it's just a REST
API, so you can use it

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:00.280
in any language you'd like.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:02.670
So here, we're calling the
analyze sentiment end point,

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:07.200
and then we're analyzing
the sentiment of our text.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.902
So let's go into a demo of
the Natural Language API.

00:08:11.902 --> 00:08:13.860
Again, I'm going to show
the product page here.

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.790
So right in the browser
on the NLAPI product page,

00:08:17.790 --> 00:08:19.500
I can enter text
directly in here.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.129
So I'm going to
enter some new text.

00:08:22.129 --> 00:08:23.670
So this is a sentence
from an article

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:26.790
about the World Cup published
just a couple of weeks ago.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.630
And we will see what the
NLAPI is able to identify.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:34.470
So we can see it extracts all
of these entities from our text.

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.289
So if the entity returns
a Wikipedia page,

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:39.940
it will link to that
page in the response.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.250
So this is one of the players.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.919
It links to the
Wikipedia page about him.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:47.326
This links to the Wikipedia
page about the French National

00:08:47.326 --> 00:08:49.950
football team, which is why it's
categorized as an organization

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:51.144
type.

00:08:51.144 --> 00:08:52.560
And then we also
get the sentiment

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:54.450
for each entity, broken
down by entities.

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:58.620
So scores a value ranging
from negative 1 to 1,

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:02.290
telling us if this entity is,
overall, positive or negative.

00:09:02.290 --> 00:09:05.220
So that's what we get
through entity analysis.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.950
Sentiment will give us the
overall aggregate sentiment

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.561
of the entire document,
along with the sentiment

00:09:10.561 --> 00:09:11.310
for each sentence.

00:09:14.390 --> 00:09:17.010
From the syntax response, we
get some additional linguistic

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:19.740
details about the text,
which you can see here,

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:23.910
parts of speech, the role
of each word in a sentence.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:26.940
And then finally, the content
classification end point

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.150
is able to identify that this
is an article about soccer

00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.540
with 80% confidence.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:33.840
It's pretty cool,
considering the words soccer,

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:36.510
football, World Cup, are not
actually explicitly mentioned

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:37.821
in the text.

00:09:37.821 --> 00:09:39.570
And you can also look
in the documentation

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.640
to see a full list of
the content categories

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:44.140
that are available
from this method.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:45.982
So that was the Natural
Language API demo.

00:09:45.982 --> 00:09:47.190
We can go back to the slides.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.090
Next, I want to highlight
one of our customers

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:56.450
that's using both the vision
and Natural Language APIs

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:57.532
in production.

00:09:57.532 --> 00:09:59.990
And for that, I want welcome
to the stage Nick Hasty, who's

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:02.030
the director of R&amp;D at GIPHY.

00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:03.604
Welcome, Nick.

00:10:03.604 --> 00:10:05.492
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:12.445
NICK HASTY: : Thanks, Sara.

00:10:12.445 --> 00:10:13.044
So, hi.

00:10:13.044 --> 00:10:13.960
My name is Nick Hasty.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.085
I'm director of research
and development for GIPHY.

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:22.930
GIPHY is about a
five-year-old startup.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:28.816
We have about 100 employees,
75 in New York, about 25 in LA.

00:10:28.816 --> 00:10:30.190
So today I'm going
to talk to you

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:34.270
about how GIPHY used the Google
Cloud Vision APIs and Google

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:37.630
Cloud Natural Language
APIs to get better

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:39.790
metadata about our
GIFs, and to also

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:44.800
increase our search experience.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:48.880
So the problems that we were
facing about two years ago,

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:51.880
we were accumulating so
many images, so many GIFs,

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.300
that the ways in which we
annotated those GIFs just

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:56.600
weren't cutting it anymore.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:10:58.000
We had an editorial team.

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:01.600
You know, they would add
tags and labels to GIFs,

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.390
but we were working in
tens of millions of GIFs.

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:06.630
And we were getting
thousands more every day.

00:11:06.630 --> 00:11:09.040
It's just not the best
use of our resources

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.290
internally to have
people slaving away

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:13.630
all day adding tags.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:16.180
At this point in time,
there was a good amount

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:20.530
of availability of
machine-learning resources

00:11:20.530 --> 00:11:23.530
for object detection in images.

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.430
We evaluated a lot
of different options,

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:27.315
including open source ones.

00:11:27.315 --> 00:11:28.690
But those have
their own problems

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:30.239
like you've got to
deploy, and you've

00:11:30.239 --> 00:11:32.530
got to do all these other
things, maintenance services,

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:33.920
and these kinds of things.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:38.080
We found that the Google Cloud
API was the best thing for us

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:39.190
overall.

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:41.660
Again, what we were trying
to do is pretty simple.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:43.660
We just had a bunch of images.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:46.160
We just wanted to figure out
what was inside those images.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:49.220
For GIPHY, knowing if
a cat is in an image

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:51.167
is actually a big
business deal for us.

00:11:51.167 --> 00:11:52.750
So we wanted to pull
those things out.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:12:00.190
In any startup, you don't
want to reinvent the wheel,

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:02.980
and you want to minimize risk.

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:05.620
So for us to not have to
train our own custom thing

00:12:05.620 --> 00:12:08.534
and to just be able to hit
an API with an HTP request

00:12:08.534 --> 00:12:09.950
and get like a
really good quality

00:12:09.950 --> 00:12:11.320
metadata about an image--

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:12.910
huge.

00:12:12.910 --> 00:12:15.510
It makes a job that
would, historically,

00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:17.950
or, you know, any time,
be it like a whole team

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:20.120
to a single person.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:23.000
So let me show you some of
the results we got for this.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:24.580
So these are A/B test results.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:28.720
On the left-hand side, you
can see improvements in CTR

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:33.430
for our search results pages
across our own, and operated.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:35.680
So the bottom line,
the dark blue line,

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:36.710
is the control group.

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:40.510
And the top line, the light
blue, is our experiment group.

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:43.390
The experiment group, we
added Google web entities

00:12:43.390 --> 00:12:46.270
into our search
algorithm as a signal.

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:47.760
Google web entities,
for us, were

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:50.946
really awesome, because not only
do they provide generic labels,

00:12:50.946 --> 00:12:53.320
since they understand the
context in which a GIF is found

00:12:53.320 --> 00:12:54.580
on the internet,
there's a lot of times

00:12:54.580 --> 00:12:55.704
like proper names in there.

00:12:55.704 --> 00:12:59.680
So you might find
celebrities, TV shows,

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:02.810
those kinds of things, which
is our bread and butter.

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:06.910
So yeah, when we did this A/B
test, you could see an 86--

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:11.500
0.86% relative
increase in CTR, which

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:15.820
is a really good metric for
us, and for any kind of CTR

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:17.320
evaluation.

00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:19.420
And then also, the
other byproduct of this

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:22.780
was we were turning
less empty searches.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:26.500
So on the right side,
those are non-zero results.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:28.330
So we're returning,
as you can see,

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:31.660
we had a higher percentage of
searches that were not empty.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:33.370
So a person would
search for something,

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:34.703
they wouldn't get anything back.

00:13:34.703 --> 00:13:36.370
Reason being, we
had more metadata

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:40.200
about our GIFs, which increased
our overall relevance.

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:42.700
Another thing, too, is like a
lot of our long-tail searches,

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:45.160
we noticed those
got much better.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:48.160
Because a lot of the stuff
was in kind of metadata limbo.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:50.770
You know, we didn't really
have good metadata around it.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:53.800
Now that we did, those things
started coming up, bubbling up,

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:54.700
in the algorithm.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:57.140
So as we scrolled down our
pages in our search results,

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:01.210
we were just seeing
better stuff all around.

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:03.000
These numbers may
seem kind of small

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.650
in terms of, like, under 1%.

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:09.890
But GIPHY serves about
5 billion GIFs a day

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:13.160
to 300 daily, active users.

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:17.440
So any increase in this
amount is substantial.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.240
OK, here's another thing we did.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.310
Hopefully, you can see this.

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:24.790
We needed to come up with
better ways to title our GIFs.

00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:26.740
We use the titles
anytime we display

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:28.930
GIFs on the website,
on our mobile apps,

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:31.270
and these other things.

00:14:31.270 --> 00:14:35.260
So the old way we did this
before we used Google Natural

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:36.970
Language processing
API was we would just

00:14:36.970 --> 00:14:40.090
get the most popular tag on
a GIF, and then we would say,

00:14:40.090 --> 00:14:42.040
most popular tag, GIF.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:44.900
Not always wrong,
but not really good.

00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:46.960
So as you can see
in the upper left,

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.840
there's a chipmunk on the
bed, that was just bed GIF;

00:14:49.840 --> 00:14:52.450
elephant jumping a rope,
that was just an animal GIF.

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:55.970
And a guy professing his
love to a woman at the door

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.850
was just a love GIF.

00:14:57.850 --> 00:15:01.810
So what we wanted to do to
increase these titles was we

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.880
came up with a bunch
of semantic structures

00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:09.210
like noun, verb,
adjective, noun, verb,

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:12.010
that we knew that would
be the best titles for us.

00:15:12.010 --> 00:15:13.640
Then we took all our tags--

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:15.250
or a subset of our tags rather.

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:17.620
We sent them to the Google
Natural Language processing

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:18.220
APIs.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:20.595
We figured out their
parts of speech.

00:15:20.595 --> 00:15:22.720
The other cool thing is
the Google Natural Language

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.630
APIs returned
Wikipedia articles,

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:28.070
and they returned
Knowledge Graph references.

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:30.220
So we were able to
cross-reference those resources

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:31.780
to figure out when
we had a tag that

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.219
was like a proper noun
or those kinds of things.

00:15:34.219 --> 00:15:36.760
So then we got that data and we
applied it to the structures.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:38.840
We started producing
titles for these images.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.580
You can see bottom left,
yawn-stretch GIF, elephant jump

00:15:42.580 --> 00:15:44.800
rope GIF, Valentine's
Day hello GIF.

00:15:44.800 --> 00:15:45.830
Way better.

00:15:45.830 --> 00:15:48.430
We were super stoked
with all that.

00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:49.690
That's it for us.

00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:50.950
Real quick, again, thanks.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:52.180
My name is Nick Hasty.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:54.440
You can get me Nick@GIPHY.com.

00:15:54.440 --> 00:15:57.070
And below that is a bit URL
that leads to a blog post

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:59.110
article on the Google
Cloud Machine Learning

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:01.410
blog where I go into more
detail about all this.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:01.910
Thank you.

00:16:08.944 --> 00:16:10.110
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks, Nick.

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:15.166
Awesome to see about customers
using these APIs in production.

00:16:15.166 --> 00:16:17.040
So a lot of times when
I present on our APIs,

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.070
the first question
I always get is:

00:16:19.070 --> 00:16:22.651
What if I want to train these
APIs on my own custom data?

00:16:22.651 --> 00:16:24.650
And for that, I'm super
excited about our newest

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:27.980
product in cloud machine
learning, Cloud AutoML, which

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:30.230
lets you train your own
custom machine-learning models

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:32.870
for vision, natural
language, and translation

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:36.950
without requiring you to
write any of the model code.

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:38.880
So this is a diagram
of how it works.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:42.490
You upload your training data,
could be images for AutoML

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:46.250
vision, documents, text,
sentences for AutoML Natural

00:16:46.250 --> 00:16:48.400
Language, and then
sentence translation pairs

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:50.220
for using AutoML Translate.

00:16:50.220 --> 00:16:54.300
AutoML provides a UI for
uploading your training data,

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:56.450
making sure your data
is labeled correctly.

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:58.130
You just click a
button to train it.

00:16:58.130 --> 00:17:00.200
And then you get access
to a custom REST API

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.910
to make predictions
on that data.

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:03.840
And AutoML will handle
scaling it for you.

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:06.050
So if your model
becomes a smash hit,

00:17:06.050 --> 00:17:09.064
you get hundreds of thousands of
requests, AutoML will scale up,

00:17:09.064 --> 00:17:11.480
and then it will also scale
all the way back down to zero,

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.980
so you only pay
for what you use.

00:17:14.980 --> 00:17:17.579
So let's get started
right with AutoML Vision.

00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:20.369
To show you AutoML Vision,
we're going to dive right

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:21.390
into a demo.

00:17:21.390 --> 00:17:25.619
And for this demo, let's pretend
that I am a meteorologist,

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:27.990
and I want to predict
weather trends and flight

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:30.550
plans from images of clouds.

00:17:30.550 --> 00:17:32.130
So this begs the
question, can we

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:33.930
use the cloud to analyze clouds?

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:35.190
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:37.720
The answer is yes.

00:17:37.720 --> 00:17:39.260
So as I was prepping
this demo, I

00:17:39.260 --> 00:17:41.710
learned that there's many
different types of clouds,

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:44.259
and they all indicate
different weather patterns.

00:17:44.259 --> 00:17:46.050
So my first thought
was, let's try this out

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:48.370
with the Vision API.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:50.890
So for us, it's easy to see
that these are, obviously,

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:52.867
two different types of clouds.

00:17:52.867 --> 00:17:54.450
But for the Vision
API, it was trained

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:56.580
on a broad range of different
categories of images.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.547
We wouldn't really expect it
to know the scientific names

00:17:59.547 --> 00:18:00.630
for these types of clouds.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:02.005
You can see that
for both of them

00:18:02.005 --> 00:18:05.490
it returns pretty much the same
label: sky, clouds, blue, et

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.600
cetera.

00:18:06.600 --> 00:18:10.790
So the answer was build a
custom model in AutoML Vision.

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:15.670
And let's jump over to the
demo to take a look at that.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:17.730
So this is the AutoML Vision UI.

00:18:17.730 --> 00:18:20.730
It takes me through every
step of building and training

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:22.180
my model.

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:24.420
So here, what we have
is the Images tab.

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:27.900
It shows me all the images that
I'm using to train my model.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:31.590
I can either create a CSV
with the URLs of my images

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:32.916
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:32.916 --> 00:18:34.290
And in the second
column, I would

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:35.373
have the associated label.

00:18:35.373 --> 00:18:38.610
Or I can upload my images
directly in the UI right here.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:40.534
So you can see in
this model, our model

00:18:40.534 --> 00:18:42.700
is going to recognize five
different types of clouds

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:43.420
as we see here.

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:47.390
And this is the number of
photos that I have for each one.

00:18:47.390 --> 00:18:50.240
So once I've got all my
data in AutoML Vision,

00:18:50.240 --> 00:18:54.607
the next step is to jump
over here to the Train tab.

00:18:54.607 --> 00:18:56.190
So this is where I
can train my model.

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.120
I can look at some metrics.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:18:59.720
So when I click
train, I can choose

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:03.150
how long I want to train
it for, right there.

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:06.380
And once my model's done
training, I'll get an email,

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:08.720
and then it's time to
look at some metrics

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:10.850
to see how my model performed.

00:19:10.850 --> 00:19:14.990
And what I want to highlight
here is the Confusion matrix.

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:16.670
So it's called a
Confusion matrix.

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:18.726
It's actually not
that confusing.

00:19:18.726 --> 00:19:20.600
What we want to see here
is a strong diagonal

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:21.480
from the top left.

00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:23.540
So this tells us the
percentage of images

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:26.940
in our test set that our model
was able to classify correctly.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:29.600
So when we uploaded all of
our images to AutoML Vision,

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:31.550
it automatically split
our images for us

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:32.674
into training and testing.

00:19:32.674 --> 00:19:35.090
So when we're building any
sort of machine-learning model,

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:36.756
we want to take the
majority of our data

00:19:36.756 --> 00:19:38.007
and use it to train the model.

00:19:38.007 --> 00:19:40.340
But then when we test the
model to see how it performed,

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:43.140
we want to use data that
it's never seen before.

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:45.840
So this is evaluating
on the test data.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.800
So we can see that it
identified 92% of my cumulus

00:19:48.800 --> 00:19:50.240
clouds correctly.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.369
Did pretty good for most
of them, but for this one,

00:19:52.369 --> 00:19:53.910
it looked like there
was some issues.

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:58.280
So for altostratus, it only
got 58% of those correct.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:01.700
Turns out it mislabelled a
lot of them as cirrus clouds.

00:20:01.700 --> 00:20:05.630
And I can click here to see
where my model got confused.

00:20:05.630 --> 00:20:08.660
So these were images
it predicted correctly.

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:10.800
These are images
that were confusing.

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:13.336
So this is where I can see
maybe these images were labeled

00:20:13.336 --> 00:20:15.710
incorrectly, maybe they're
confusing images that I should

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:17.720
just delete from my data set.

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:19.670
This UI helps you go in
and see where you need

00:20:19.670 --> 00:20:23.390
to improve your training data.

00:20:23.390 --> 00:20:25.310
So that's the Evaluate tab.

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:27.770
And the most fun
part of this process

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:30.690
is generating predictions once
you've got a trained model.

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:32.190
There's a couple
of ways to do this.

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:35.000
I'm going to show you
how to do it in the UI.

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:38.086
So for this, we're
going to upload images

00:20:38.086 --> 00:20:39.460
of two different
types of clouds,

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.750
and we're going to see
what our model predicts.

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:43.740
And there we go.

00:20:43.740 --> 00:20:46.010
So this is a cumulonimbus cloud.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:49.482
It's 96% confident that it
predicted that one correctly.

00:20:49.482 --> 00:20:50.690
This is a mean type of cloud.

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:54.440
You probably wouldn't want to
see it if you were on a plane.

00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:55.640
This one is a cirrus cloud.

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:58.130
It got 96% confidence.

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:00.859
So you can try out your
predictions in the UI.

00:21:00.859 --> 00:21:02.900
It's a great way to see
how our model works right

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:04.274
after it's been trained.

00:21:04.274 --> 00:21:05.690
But chances are,
you probably want

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:08.020
to build an application that's
going to make prediction

00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:09.380
requests to your trained model.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:12.050
So you get access to
this custom API endpoint.

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:13.700
Only you have access
to this, or anyone

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:15.410
that you share
your project with.

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:18.209
And let's say I decide to add
a bunch more images to improve

00:21:18.209 --> 00:21:19.250
the accuracy of my model.

00:21:19.250 --> 00:21:20.494
I train a new version.

00:21:20.494 --> 00:21:22.160
All I need to do, or
all the application

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:24.800
developer needs to do
is change this model ID

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:27.050
in the API request.

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:29.472
So that is AutoML Vision.

00:21:29.472 --> 00:21:30.680
We can go back to the slides.

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:37.206
So we announced AutoML
Vision earlier this year.

00:21:37.206 --> 00:21:38.580
And some companies
that have been

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:40.590
participating in the alpha--

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:41.910
first is Disney.

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:44.370
Disney built a model to
identify specific Disney

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:47.340
characters, product
categories, and colors

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:48.900
that were specific to them.

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:51.480
And they use that to improve
the accuracy of their search

00:21:51.480 --> 00:21:52.920
results.

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:55.620
Urban Outfitters is
a clothing company.

00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:58.980
They built a similar model
to identify characteristics

00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:01.350
in clothing, so types
of patterns in clothing,

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:03.660
types of neckline
styles, things like that.

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:05.220
They're using it to
automatically tag

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.890
products and improve
their recommendations

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:09.010
in search results.

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:10.980
So those are some examples
of companies using

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:14.180
AutoML Vision in production.

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:17.600
But Vision is just one
domain of machine learning.

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:22.090
So what about other
types of data, like text?

00:22:22.090 --> 00:22:25.872
That's where AutoML Natural
Language comes into play.

00:22:25.872 --> 00:22:27.955
So I wanted to start out
by talking about a couple

00:22:27.955 --> 00:22:30.880
of potential use cases of
AutoML Natural Language--

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:33.215
why you might want a
custom text classifier.

00:22:33.215 --> 00:22:35.590
So these are just a couple of
examples that I thought of.

00:22:35.590 --> 00:22:38.470
The first was, let's say you've
got some data like a Stack

00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:40.540
Overflow question and
you want your model

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:42.880
to automatically tag that
question with an associated

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:44.436
programming language.

00:22:44.436 --> 00:22:46.060
Or maybe you want to
build a model that

00:22:46.060 --> 00:22:48.400
takes a news headline,
is able to predict

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:49.750
where that headline came from.

00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:53.050
I actually built a model to do
this on AutoML Natural Language

00:22:53.050 --> 00:22:55.120
and the results were
pretty impressive.

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:58.210
One other example is, let's
say that you want to--

00:22:58.210 --> 00:22:59.994
you've got doctor's
notes about a patient,

00:22:59.994 --> 00:23:02.410
and maybe you want to predict
the possible conditions that

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:05.830
would be associated
with those symptoms.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:07.780
To show you how this
works, I'm going

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:10.120
to use a demo that you may
have seen in the keynote

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:12.190
with a data set
from Donors Choose.

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:13.690
So I found this
data set on Kaggle.

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:16.044
Kaggle is a data science
platform that's part of Google.

00:23:16.044 --> 00:23:17.710
If you haven't heard
of it, I definitely

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:18.920
recommend checking it out.

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:21.130
They've got a lot of
great data sets on there.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:24.250
So this data set includes
data from Donors Choose.

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:27.340
And what they do is they
match teachers with donors.

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:30.850
So teachers that are looking for
resources for their classroom

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:32.890
will create need requests.

00:23:32.890 --> 00:23:35.860
They have volunteers manually
categorizing those requests.

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:37.784
And then they use
those categories

00:23:37.784 --> 00:23:39.700
to route their requests
to relevant donors who

00:23:39.700 --> 00:23:41.890
are interested in
donating to the project.

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:44.050
So they posted this
data set to Kaggle

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:45.880
to see if the community
of data scientists

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:48.360
and machine-learning experts
could build them an automated

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:48.860
solution.

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:52.500
So I thought I would try it out
with AutoML Natural Language.

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.106
So let's go to the demo.

00:23:57.230 --> 00:24:00.500
So as you'll see, the UI
for AutoML Natural Language

00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:03.590
looks very similar to
the UI for AutoML Vision.

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:06.080
So here we have all
of the text inputs

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:11.320
to our model and their
associated categories.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:14.740
And these are all the possible
categories in our data set.

00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:16.920
So we can see all
the categories here.

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:22.480
And the Train tab looks
the same as AutoML Vision.

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:25.146
And we can take a look
at the Confusion matrix

00:24:25.146 --> 00:24:28.211
to evaluate the accuracy
of how our model performed.

00:24:28.211 --> 00:24:29.460
We can see it did pretty well.

00:24:29.460 --> 00:24:31.620
We got, again, a strong
diagonal down the top

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:34.500
left, indicating
that it categorized

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:39.660
most of the text items from
our test set correctly.

00:24:39.660 --> 00:24:42.170
So what we want to do
next is run a prediction

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:44.600
on some new text that
hasn't been categorized yet.

00:24:44.600 --> 00:24:46.970
So we'll head over here
to the Predict tab.

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:50.060
And I'm going to take the
following need request.

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:52.400
Let me zoom in so
you can see that.

00:24:52.400 --> 00:24:54.290
It says, my students
need aquarium lighting

00:24:54.290 --> 00:24:57.990
for our environmental
clownfish aquaculture project.

00:24:57.990 --> 00:25:01.010
So this should be
classified as lab equipment.

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:04.850
And let's see if our model
is able to identify it.

00:25:04.850 --> 00:25:05.520
So there we go.

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:09.380
We can see our model identified
this as lab equipment with 98%

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:12.310
competence, which is
pretty cool considering

00:25:12.310 --> 00:25:14.920
it's not totally obvious
from the text itself

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:18.620
that this is the category
that should be assigned.

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:20.510
So again, similar
to AutoML Vision,

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:23.420
we get a custom
REST API that we can

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:27.697
use to make predictions on our
trained natural language model.

00:25:27.697 --> 00:25:29.030
So we can go back to the slides.

00:25:34.654 --> 00:25:36.680
We go back to the slides.

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:38.680
There we go.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:41.760
So next, I'm moving right
along to AutoML Translate.

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:43.290
Before I dive into
AutoML Translate,

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:45.690
I want to talk about why
domain-specific translation is

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:46.710
important.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:49.830
Because oftentimes, using
our Cloud Translation API

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:51.997
works great for many use cases.

00:25:51.997 --> 00:25:53.580
But there are certain
situations where

00:25:53.580 --> 00:25:55.320
you do need domain-specific
translation.

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:57.330
Maybe you have a lot of
industry-specific jargon

00:25:57.330 --> 00:25:58.810
that you need to translate.

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:01.620
So I'm going to use this
error message as an example.

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:03.930
So the message says,
device driver software

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:06.030
was not successfully installed.

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:08.632
So let's say I wanted
to summarize this,

00:26:08.632 --> 00:26:10.090
focusing here on
the word "driver."

00:26:10.090 --> 00:26:13.500
Let's say I summarize this to
say, the driver is not working

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.700
and now I want to
translate that.

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:17.970
So taken out of context
of the error message,

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:20.710
this actually might apply
to a number of things.

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:22.860
So the sentence is the
driver is not working.

00:26:22.860 --> 00:26:24.990
It could be referring
to a broken golf club.

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:26.880
My driver club is broken.

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:29.330
It could also be
referring to maybe a taxi

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:30.547
driver is on strike.

00:26:30.547 --> 00:26:31.380
They're not working.

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.870
The driver is not working.

00:26:33.870 --> 00:26:38.070
Or it could apply to
the software use case

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:39.580
that we saw on
the previous side.

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.880
So this is an example
of why you might

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:44.640
need a domain-specific
translation model.

00:26:47.027 --> 00:26:49.360
To show you a demo, I'm going
to use a data set provided

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:51.110
by one of our customers.

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:52.952
Their name is
welocalize. welocalize

00:26:52.952 --> 00:26:54.670
is one of the
largest localization

00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:55.630
providers in the US.

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:57.129
And they help their
customers, which

00:26:57.129 --> 00:27:00.040
span many different industries,
localize their content

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:02.187
for global audiences.

00:27:02.187 --> 00:27:04.270
And so we're going to
highlight data sets provided

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:07.090
by two of their customers
that they have experimented

00:27:07.090 --> 00:27:08.610
with AutoML Translate for us.

00:27:08.610 --> 00:27:11.680
So those are OpenTable
and Blackboard.

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:14.440
So welocalize has a lot
of localization expertise,

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:16.810
because when you build an
AutoML Translate model,

00:27:16.810 --> 00:27:18.400
you need to provide
it with thousands

00:27:18.400 --> 00:27:20.500
of sentence pairs
translating from a source

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:21.530
to a target language.

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:22.990
So they have a team
of experts that

00:27:22.990 --> 00:27:25.720
helps gather that training
data and analyze the quality

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:28.030
of the resulting model.

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:30.740
So what OpenTable did, they've
been experimenting with AutoML

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:31.930
Translate during the alpha.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:34.330
They built a model that
translates from English

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:36.340
to German to
translate restaurant

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:40.990
reviews, descriptions, and
UI elements on the site.

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:43.240
For those of you that aren't
familiar with Blackboard,

00:27:43.240 --> 00:27:44.406
it's an e-learning platform.

00:27:44.406 --> 00:27:47.650
It helps teachers post
homework in their courses.

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:49.540
It lets students
engage in discussion,

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:52.130
and look up their
grades, things like that.

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:54.340
So they built a model to
translate from English

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:57.460
to Spanish to
translate user guides

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:59.932
and you UI elements
on their site.

00:27:59.932 --> 00:28:01.390
So I noticed both
of these examples

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:04.079
translate from English into
a non-English language.

00:28:04.079 --> 00:28:06.370
To show you a demo, I've
actually reversed the sentence

00:28:06.370 --> 00:28:08.470
pairs so that they
translate into English

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.420
so that you can see
the benefits you get

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:12.211
from using the custom model.

00:28:12.211 --> 00:28:13.960
The accuracy of the
reverse sentence pairs

00:28:13.960 --> 00:28:16.534
is going to be pretty similar
to the original models

00:28:16.534 --> 00:28:17.200
that they built.

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:22.030
So if we could go to the demo.

00:28:22.030 --> 00:28:24.670
I'm going to show you a demo
for both OpenTable Blackboard.

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:26.480
They have different
data sets, obviously.

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:28.090
So this is what
the training data

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:30.070
looks like in AutoML Translate.

00:28:30.070 --> 00:28:32.110
So here we have the
German-to-English sentence

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:32.610
pairs.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:34.790
This is the OpenTable example.

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:37.989
So they're translating UI
elements, restaurant reviews,

00:28:37.989 --> 00:28:39.280
and descriptions on their site.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:44.530
So we go over here
to the Train tab.

00:28:44.530 --> 00:28:46.400
You'll see what's
called a blue score.

00:28:46.400 --> 00:28:49.390
And this is a score that's used
by neural machine translation

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:52.732
experts to evaluate the
accuracy of a train model.

00:28:52.732 --> 00:28:54.190
So what you want
to see here is you

00:28:54.190 --> 00:28:56.710
want to see a higher blue
score than the base model.

00:28:56.710 --> 00:28:59.930
This is what the Translation API
would provide on the same data.

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.290
So here we got a
20-point improvement.

00:29:02.290 --> 00:29:05.890
And what we want to do here
is generate a prediction

00:29:05.890 --> 00:29:08.770
on some new text.

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:11.810
So I'm going to enter a
restaurant review in German.

00:29:11.810 --> 00:29:14.222
And what we'll see up here is
what we get from our custom

00:29:14.222 --> 00:29:15.680
model, and then we
can compare that

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:18.290
to what we get-- this is
the same exact response we'd

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:20.936
get if we used Google Translate
on this same sentence.

00:29:20.936 --> 00:29:21.810
So let's take a look.

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:24.101
So the first sentence, "Service
was nice and friendly,"

00:29:24.101 --> 00:29:25.890
this is actually the same.

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:28.089
So you don't always need a
custom translation model,

00:29:28.089 --> 00:29:29.630
but there are certain
use cases where

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:32.564
it leads to a lot of
improvements in accuracy.

00:29:32.564 --> 00:29:33.980
So here we see
this was translated

00:29:33.980 --> 00:29:37.230
to "drinks were upscale," which
is a much better translation

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:38.814
than the word "lifted."

00:29:38.814 --> 00:29:39.980
And then right here it says.

00:29:39.980 --> 00:29:42.020
"We highly recommend,"
which, again,

00:29:42.020 --> 00:29:45.282
is a better translation than
"we can only recommend."

00:29:45.282 --> 00:29:46.740
So we get these
small improvements,

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:49.270
which do lead to a lot
of accuracy improvements

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:50.300
in the end.

00:29:50.300 --> 00:29:52.760
So that's the OpenTable example.

00:29:52.760 --> 00:29:55.100
Looking at a different
domain, let's take

00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:58.140
a look at the Blackboard model.

00:29:58.140 --> 00:30:02.210
So here I'm going to enter
some text in Spanish,

00:30:02.210 --> 00:30:05.139
and we'll translate it, and
we'll compare the results here.

00:30:05.139 --> 00:30:06.680
So here, we notice
right off the bat,

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:08.990
we get a better translation
here of this word.

00:30:08.990 --> 00:30:11.030
So "graders" is
obviously a term that's

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.515
used across Blackboard's
application.

00:30:13.515 --> 00:30:15.140
The baseline model
would translate this

00:30:15.140 --> 00:30:18.770
to "qualifiers," which doesn't
really make sense in this case.

00:30:18.770 --> 00:30:21.140
"Retention Center"
is a proper noun

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:24.170
in their platform, which is
translated more accurately

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:25.280
by the custom model.

00:30:25.280 --> 00:30:27.800
And over here we
have "swiping" versus

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:31.585
"scrolling," "submission"
versus "shipments."

00:30:31.585 --> 00:30:32.960
So "submission"
is what they want

00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:35.990
to call submitting
something in their platform.

00:30:35.990 --> 00:30:37.730
And then finally,
"submission listing,"

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:42.040
which is a better translation
than "ship list page."

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:44.780
So you can see for any
domain-specific translation use

00:30:44.780 --> 00:30:47.630
case, you do get a lot
of quality improvements

00:30:47.630 --> 00:30:50.600
by building a custom model
using your own training

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:52.462
data for that.

00:30:52.462 --> 00:30:53.795
So we can go back to the slides.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:59.600
And then moving into the
last part of the talk,

00:30:59.600 --> 00:31:02.000
what if you have
a custom task that

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:05.691
requires more control over
your model type and its inputs?

00:31:05.691 --> 00:31:07.940
So some examples of this
are, let's say you've already

00:31:07.940 --> 00:31:08.815
built your own model.

00:31:08.815 --> 00:31:11.960
You built a model in
TensorFlow or scikit-learn

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:13.989
and you want to
train that model.

00:31:13.989 --> 00:31:16.280
So maybe you want to train
it on Google Cloud Platform.

00:31:16.280 --> 00:31:17.450
Or maybe you want
to scale the way

00:31:17.450 --> 00:31:19.824
to serve that model to handle
lots and lots of prediction

00:31:19.824 --> 00:31:20.577
requests.

00:31:20.577 --> 00:31:22.160
Another example is,
let's say you want

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:23.370
to build a regression model.

00:31:23.370 --> 00:31:25.910
So that's a model that will
output numerical values.

00:31:25.910 --> 00:31:27.860
So maybe you have a
text input and you

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:31.760
want your model to assign some
sort of rating to that text.

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:34.150
Or maybe you have a model
that has multiple inputs.

00:31:34.150 --> 00:31:36.590
So maybe it takes images
and text as input,

00:31:36.590 --> 00:31:39.450
and then outputs some sort
of prediction based on that.

00:31:39.450 --> 00:31:41.750
So these are all use cases
where you want more control

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:44.330
over your model.

00:31:44.330 --> 00:31:47.200
And for that we have Cloud
Machine Learning Engine, which

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:49.960
is Google's managed platform
for building, training,

00:31:49.960 --> 00:31:53.470
and serving custom models
using your own data.

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.245
So it's our fully managed
platform for machine learning.

00:31:56.245 --> 00:31:58.120
It supports a couple of
different frameworks.

00:31:58.120 --> 00:32:01.120
So it supports TensorFlow,
Keras, and then most recently,

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:04.630
scikit-learn an XGBoost
models for serving.

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:07.330
So ML Engine is a way for
you to train your model.

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:10.450
So you can take advantage
of using GPUs or TPUs, which

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:12.545
stands for tensor
processing units, which

00:32:12.545 --> 00:32:14.920
will help you accelerate your
machine learning workloads.

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:16.700
So you can use it for
training your models.

00:32:16.700 --> 00:32:18.533
You can also use it for
serving your models.

00:32:18.533 --> 00:32:20.170
So once you've got
a trained model,

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:22.450
you can serve it
on Cloud ML Engine,

00:32:22.450 --> 00:32:24.550
and then you get access
to an easy-to-use API

00:32:24.550 --> 00:32:26.980
to make predictions
on your trained model.

00:32:26.980 --> 00:32:28.930
One of the great
things about ML Engine

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:30.380
is that there's no lock in.

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:31.240
So let's say that
I want to train

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:32.650
my model on my
own infrastructure

00:32:32.650 --> 00:32:34.330
but then I want a
way to serve it.

00:32:34.330 --> 00:32:38.290
I can upload it to ML Engine
for serving, or vise versa.

00:32:38.290 --> 00:32:40.150
I could train it on
ML Engine and then

00:32:40.150 --> 00:32:42.934
serve it somewhere else.

00:32:42.934 --> 00:32:44.850
Some companies that are
using Machine Learning

00:32:44.850 --> 00:32:47.370
Engine in production,
Rolls-Royce

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:50.070
has a TensorFlow model that
they're using for the company's

00:32:50.070 --> 00:32:51.290
Marine Division.

00:32:51.290 --> 00:32:52.990
And they have an
object detection model,

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:55.115
which means that it returns
the location of objects

00:32:55.115 --> 00:32:56.940
in an image, similar
to the cat example

00:32:56.940 --> 00:32:59.070
that I showed earlier
in the presentation.

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:01.020
They're using that
to identify and track

00:33:01.020 --> 00:33:04.110
the objects that a vessel
can encounter at sea.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:06.570
Another example is Ocado.

00:33:06.570 --> 00:33:10.344
Ocado is the UK'S largest
grocery delivery service.

00:33:10.344 --> 00:33:11.760
As you can imagine,
they get tons,

00:33:11.760 --> 00:33:14.190
and tons of customer support
emails on a daily basis.

00:33:14.190 --> 00:33:16.539
So they built a custom
model to optimize

00:33:16.539 --> 00:33:17.830
how they handle those requests.

00:33:17.830 --> 00:33:20.550
So some requests don't
require any response at all.

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:22.560
Some requests are very urgent.

00:33:22.560 --> 00:33:25.230
So they're able to route
requests appropriately using

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.480
that custom model.

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:30.264
And to show you a
demo of ML Engine,

00:33:30.264 --> 00:33:31.680
I'm going to bring
up my teammate,

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:32.950
Zach Akil to the stage.

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:37.129
Welcome, Zach.

00:33:40.482 --> 00:33:41.919
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:44.800 --> 00:33:46.550
ZACH AKIL: Thank you, Sara.

00:33:46.550 --> 00:33:48.760
If we could switch
back to the slides.

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:49.690
Have we got--

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:56.020
Awesome.

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:57.040
So I'm Zach Akil.

00:33:57.040 --> 00:33:59.450
I'm one of the developer
advocates for Google Cloud

00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:00.610
Platform.

00:34:00.610 --> 00:34:02.560
And I'm really
excited to show of how

00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:07.360
we can use ML Engine to deploy
a custom model that we've built,

00:34:07.360 --> 00:34:09.639
and then how we can
also integrate that

00:34:09.639 --> 00:34:11.565
into an actual app.

00:34:11.565 --> 00:34:13.690
So let's just jump right
in and look at the problem

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:15.920
that our app is going to solve.

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:16.650
So this fun.

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:20.480
On given a movie description,
can we predict the genre?

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:23.530
So this is an example of a
natural language problem.

00:34:23.530 --> 00:34:26.139
But as Sara was saying,
we could have easily done

00:34:26.139 --> 00:34:29.320
an image-based problem or
a numeric-based problem.

00:34:29.320 --> 00:34:32.320
We've just chosen this one
because it's a bit of fun.

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:35.300
But it will be a bit special,
so let's look at an example.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:36.760
So here's the description.

00:34:36.760 --> 00:34:39.239
I believe it's one
of the Ocean's films.

00:34:39.239 --> 00:34:40.360
It's got crime in it.

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:41.510
It's got action.

00:34:41.510 --> 00:34:44.719
So for our model, instead of
predicting just a single genre,

00:34:44.719 --> 00:34:46.510
we're actually going
to build it so that it

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:48.352
predicts multiple genres.

00:34:48.352 --> 00:34:50.560
So this is an example of a
multi-label classification

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:51.429
problem.

00:34:51.429 --> 00:34:53.920
And ML Engine will handle
this, like I said before,

00:34:53.920 --> 00:34:56.290
because it doesn't really
mind what kind of problem

00:34:56.290 --> 00:34:57.206
we're trying to solve.

00:34:57.206 --> 00:34:59.700
It will just take the
models that we built.

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:02.280
So we've actually went to when
we've built a couple of models

00:35:02.280 --> 00:35:04.150
to solve this problem.

00:35:04.150 --> 00:35:06.312
And if you want to
have a go at solving

00:35:06.312 --> 00:35:07.770
that kind of
movie-related problem,

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:10.060
we've actually used this
data set from Kaggle.

00:35:10.060 --> 00:35:11.351
It's a really, really good one.

00:35:11.351 --> 00:35:13.740
It's got like over
40,000 movies,

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:15.295
and we were picking genres.

00:35:15.295 --> 00:35:16.920
And they're got loads
of other features

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:17.920
that we didn't even use.

00:35:17.920 --> 00:35:21.090
So I really recommend
using that data set.

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:23.940
For the actual app
structure that we built,

00:35:23.940 --> 00:35:26.760
we aimed to build an app
that's as simple as possible,

00:35:26.760 --> 00:35:29.220
but one that's also
completely scalable.

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:32.331
So we built a front end
that's just static HTML.

00:35:32.331 --> 00:35:33.580
You'll see it in a little bit.

00:35:33.580 --> 00:35:35.580
It's a really simple
text box with a button

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:36.840
that you just click.

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:39.621
And we've actually just thrown
that up onto the Cloud Storage.

00:35:39.621 --> 00:35:41.370
So Cloud Storage has
a really cool feature

00:35:41.370 --> 00:35:43.830
where you can just throw up
an HTML file, click a button

00:35:43.830 --> 00:35:46.890
to make a public link, and it'll
create a public URL that anyone

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:49.420
can just go to, and it will
render it as a web page.

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:51.932
So we've just used that
to host our front end.

00:35:51.932 --> 00:35:54.390
And obviously, the back end is
going to be Cloud ML Engine.

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:56.723
That's going to do the heavy
lifting of actually hosting

00:35:56.723 --> 00:35:57.900
our deployed models.

00:35:57.900 --> 00:36:00.840
And more specifically, the
models that we've built

00:36:00.840 --> 00:36:04.620
have been a combination of
scikit-learn and TensorFlow.

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:07.230
So you're probably already a
bit familiar with TensorFlow.

00:36:07.230 --> 00:36:09.830
It's Google's deep
learning framework.

00:36:09.830 --> 00:36:12.460
But is anyone in the room
familiar with scikit-learn?

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:15.130
Got any scikit-learn--
OK, got like a handful.

00:36:15.130 --> 00:36:19.290
So scikit-learn is actually a
really comprehensive machine

00:36:19.290 --> 00:36:21.840
learning library, but
it kind of specializes

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:25.060
in the parts of machine learning
that aren't deep learning.

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:28.470
So it has a lot of algorithms
for clustering, for decision

00:36:28.470 --> 00:36:31.200
trees, and we're just
going to show you

00:36:31.200 --> 00:36:33.080
how you can combine
a couple of models,

00:36:33.080 --> 00:36:35.580
and why that can be advantageous
when you're trying to build

00:36:35.580 --> 00:36:38.150
machine-learning algorithms.

00:36:38.150 --> 00:36:40.750
So we've got our back end,
and we've got our front end.

00:36:40.750 --> 00:36:45.350
How will we get predictions from
our front end to our back end?

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:50.230
We can send requests directly
from a JavaScript front end

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:52.090
to ML Engine.

00:36:52.090 --> 00:36:54.880
The one restriction in
that is that all requests

00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:56.690
have to be authenticated.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:59.590
So we would have to build into
our front end a way for a user

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:01.450
to log in with their
Google credentials

00:37:01.450 --> 00:37:04.160
and approve them
to our own model.

00:37:04.160 --> 00:37:06.010
So that is actually a
really good solution

00:37:06.010 --> 00:37:08.560
if you want to restrict
the users of your model

00:37:08.560 --> 00:37:11.907
to be specific, so from
a certain user group.

00:37:11.907 --> 00:37:14.240
But for our one, we want it
to be as simple as possible.

00:37:14.240 --> 00:37:16.656
So we want users to be able
just go in, and without having

00:37:16.656 --> 00:37:18.470
to log in, get predictions.

00:37:18.470 --> 00:37:21.010
So that's where we
have a Cloud Function.

00:37:21.010 --> 00:37:23.500
So we've built a really
simple Cloud Function.

00:37:23.500 --> 00:37:26.020
All it does is it takes
whatever prediction

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:27.010
we're going to make--

00:37:27.010 --> 00:37:29.570
so it's going to take the
description of the movie,

00:37:29.570 --> 00:37:32.170
and it's going to just
slap on project credentials

00:37:32.170 --> 00:37:34.050
and send it off to
Cloud ML Engine.

00:37:34.050 --> 00:37:35.800
So it's not changing any data.

00:37:35.800 --> 00:37:37.870
And a way you can
think about this

00:37:37.870 --> 00:37:42.430
is it's providing an
authentication sugar glazing

00:37:42.430 --> 00:37:44.650
on a prediction-request donut.

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:46.275
So it's not actually
changing any data.

00:37:46.275 --> 00:37:47.733
It's just taking
the requests we're

00:37:47.733 --> 00:37:49.524
going to make, putting
it on credentials.

00:37:49.524 --> 00:37:51.940
And Cloud Functions actually
come with default credentials

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:53.420
for your project.

00:37:53.420 --> 00:37:55.980
So they're really useful for
doing this sort of thing.

00:37:55.980 --> 00:37:59.430
So let's actually stop talking
about it and just show it.

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:01.690
So we can switch
over to the demo.

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:05.100
So here is the Cloud
ML Engine dashboard.

00:38:05.100 --> 00:38:07.350
So we can see our model
here and we can actually

00:38:07.350 --> 00:38:11.100
look at our app, which is here.

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:12.860
So as I said, it was
a very simple app.

00:38:12.860 --> 00:38:15.270
So it's just got a movie
description and a button.

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:17.980
So let's throw in a
movie description here.

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:20.540
So we've got this one.

00:38:20.540 --> 00:38:22.882
This is one of the
Star Wars films.

00:38:22.882 --> 00:38:24.590
Does anyone have any
predictions for what

00:38:24.590 --> 00:38:25.548
genre it's going to be?

00:38:25.548 --> 00:38:27.542
Just shout them out.

00:38:27.542 --> 00:38:28.251
AUDIENCE: Sci-fi.

00:38:28.251 --> 00:38:29.125
OK, we've got sci-fi.

00:38:29.125 --> 00:38:29.970
We've got action.

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:32.224
OK, let's see.

00:38:32.224 --> 00:38:34.640
So it's going to send a request
off to our Cloud Function.

00:38:34.640 --> 00:38:35.810
That's going to go
off to ML Engine

00:38:35.810 --> 00:38:37.610
and it's going to come
back with the genres.

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:39.693
So you can see here it's
predicted action, science

00:38:39.693 --> 00:38:41.970
fiction, thriller-- is
Star Wars a thriller?

00:38:41.970 --> 00:38:42.470
Maybe.

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:44.480
I don't know.

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:45.740
So we're making predictions.

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:47.120
That's the front end.

00:38:47.120 --> 00:38:48.530
Let's look back at the back end.

00:38:48.530 --> 00:38:50.700
So we can go back
to Cloud ML Engine.

00:38:50.700 --> 00:38:52.280
So you can see
we've got our model.

00:38:52.280 --> 00:38:53.580
It shows the default version.

00:38:53.580 --> 00:38:56.038
So keep note of that because
we'll come back to that later.

00:38:56.038 --> 00:38:57.870
So default version
is version one.

00:38:57.870 --> 00:39:00.110
We can look at our model
and see that we actually

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:01.420
have two versions of our model.

00:39:01.420 --> 00:39:02.920
We have version one
and version two.

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:05.770
So when I-- talking about how we
built a model with scikit-learn

00:39:05.770 --> 00:39:07.670
and a model of
TensorFlow, version one

00:39:07.670 --> 00:39:09.560
is our scikit-learn
model, and version two

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:10.940
is our TensorFlow model.

00:39:10.940 --> 00:39:13.857
So we can go back to our app,
and we can get a prediction

00:39:13.857 --> 00:39:14.940
from our TensorFlow model.

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:18.659
So I've just built in a
little UI here, and boom.

00:39:18.659 --> 00:39:20.950
So that's getting predictions
from a scikit-learn model

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:23.201
alongside getting predictions
from a TensorFlow model.

00:39:23.201 --> 00:39:25.033
And you can see here
we've got some numbers.

00:39:25.033 --> 00:39:27.160
So the TensorFlow model,
rather than it returning

00:39:27.160 --> 00:39:29.560
just the flags of
which genre it is,

00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:32.180
it actually also
returns probabilities.

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:34.370
So we've just displayed
those as well.

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:38.100
And when we're making requests,
although I've showed it with

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:41.710
here with some radio buttons,
all I would do is I would

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:43.820
specify the model
name-- so in this case,

00:39:43.820 --> 00:39:45.460
we have a model named movies--

00:39:45.460 --> 00:39:46.820
and get prediction.

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:48.820
And that's going to go
off to our default model.

00:39:48.820 --> 00:39:50.290
So that's where the
scikit-learn model.

00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:52.915
So that's why we've got the same
tags as our scikit-learn model

00:39:52.915 --> 00:39:56.060
because version one
was our default model.

00:39:56.060 --> 00:39:57.970
So if we wanted to
be more specific,

00:39:57.970 --> 00:40:00.980
we could say get from versions--

00:40:00.980 --> 00:40:04.700
V1-- and that's also going to
go from our scikit-learn model--

00:40:04.700 --> 00:40:07.834
and then version two is
our TensorFlow model.

00:40:07.834 --> 00:40:09.250
So it goes to show
you how easy it

00:40:09.250 --> 00:40:11.740
is to just switch between
versions of a model

00:40:11.740 --> 00:40:14.761
when you're making predictions
on the application side.

00:40:14.761 --> 00:40:16.510
So that's version one,
that's version two,

00:40:16.510 --> 00:40:18.640
let's look at version three.

00:40:18.640 --> 00:40:21.590
And whoops, we don't
have version three.

00:40:21.590 --> 00:40:24.260
So let's actually go
create version three.

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:30.310
So over in our dashboard here,
we can go into the Cloud Shell.

00:40:30.310 --> 00:40:33.400
It's going to boot up a
little mini instance for us

00:40:33.400 --> 00:40:35.900
to interact with the Cloud.

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:39.540
And when it's up, we'll be
able to use the Google Cloud

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:43.170
API to build all our models.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:44.370
So here's the dashboard.

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:46.140
Here's the instance shell.

00:40:46.140 --> 00:40:50.880
We can use G cloud, and
it's just ML Engine.

00:40:50.880 --> 00:40:55.170
And for example, we can just
list the models that we have.

00:40:55.170 --> 00:40:57.520
Hopefully I've
spelled everything.

00:40:57.520 --> 00:40:59.790
And it should show one
model called movies.

00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:01.026
Make sense?

00:41:01.026 --> 00:41:02.900
Now let's create a new
version of that model.

00:41:02.900 --> 00:41:04.990
So if we go over to
our Cloud Storage--

00:41:04.990 --> 00:41:08.000
I've actually created a really
simple decision-tree model

00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:10.930
in sk-learn, and it's
just sitting here

00:41:10.930 --> 00:41:12.250
in our cloud storage.

00:41:12.250 --> 00:41:14.655
So I'll run the command.

00:41:14.655 --> 00:41:16.780
I've cheated a bit here,
so I've copied and pasted.

00:41:16.780 --> 00:41:19.600
I've made it before, but I'll
talk through what it does.

00:41:19.600 --> 00:41:22.100
So we'll run it.

00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:24.910
And what we're doing,
we're calling ML Engine.

00:41:24.910 --> 00:41:27.419
We're saying, OK, you're
going to create a new version.

00:41:27.419 --> 00:41:28.960
We're going to call
our version tree.

00:41:28.960 --> 00:41:30.220
So our previous
versions were called

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:31.090
version one, version two.

00:41:31.090 --> 00:41:32.673
We could have called
it version three,

00:41:32.673 --> 00:41:35.756
but might as well mess with
the application engineers that

00:41:35.756 --> 00:41:37.630
are going to build this
and make inconsistent

00:41:37.630 --> 00:41:39.410
naming conventions.

00:41:39.410 --> 00:41:40.849
So we'll call it tree.

00:41:40.849 --> 00:41:42.640
We said, OK, it's going
to be a new version

00:41:42.640 --> 00:41:44.412
for our model called movies.

00:41:44.412 --> 00:41:46.120
The framework's going
to be scikit-learn.

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:47.110
Make sense?

00:41:47.110 --> 00:41:49.560
The runtime version is 1.5.

00:41:49.560 --> 00:41:51.430
And that is the runtime
version of ML Engine

00:41:51.430 --> 00:41:52.492
that we're going to use.

00:41:52.492 --> 00:41:53.950
And the reason why
we've chosen 1.5

00:41:53.950 --> 00:41:57.220
is because that's the one
that supports Python 3

00:41:57.220 --> 00:41:59.590
models that we've built. So
our scikit-learn model, when

00:41:59.590 --> 00:42:01.840
we were building it, we were
building it using Python.

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:03.400
And it was in a
version of Python 3.

00:42:03.400 --> 00:42:06.100
And so we tell it, OK,
use runtime version 1.5

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:08.830
because that supports
version three.

00:42:08.830 --> 00:42:11.980
And then we've
specified, use Python 3.

00:42:11.980 --> 00:42:14.601
And then we've just told it
where the model is actually

00:42:14.601 --> 00:42:15.100
stored.

00:42:15.100 --> 00:42:17.308
We stored it in that bucket
that I showed you before.

00:42:17.308 --> 00:42:20.000
In fact, you can see it here.

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:21.950
And it's uploading now.

00:42:21.950 --> 00:42:25.230
So what's really
exciting about why

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:27.725
you can upload a model
with just one command,

00:42:27.725 --> 00:42:30.080
it goes back to the
sort of best practice

00:42:30.080 --> 00:42:31.890
with machine learning
at the moment.

00:42:31.890 --> 00:42:35.510
And that is this kind of
iterative, leap-frogging idea

00:42:35.510 --> 00:42:37.190
when you're building models.

00:42:37.190 --> 00:42:39.720
So the idea is that you build
a really, really simple model

00:42:39.720 --> 00:42:41.810
to begin with, even if
it's underperforming.

00:42:41.810 --> 00:42:44.120
And then stop, put
that to the side,

00:42:44.120 --> 00:42:46.490
and then build a second model
to try to beat that model.

00:42:46.490 --> 00:42:48.680
And you sort of leapfrog
with your models.

00:42:48.680 --> 00:42:49.580
So this is great.

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:50.720
This is a great idea.

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:54.680
Except before you just build a
model and put off to the side,

00:42:54.680 --> 00:42:57.880
but now, because deploying
is just one command,

00:42:57.880 --> 00:43:00.650
you're able to build the
model and deploy the model.

00:43:00.650 --> 00:43:02.150
And what's really
is cool about this

00:43:02.150 --> 00:43:04.352
is that for
application developers,

00:43:04.352 --> 00:43:05.810
they can immediately
start building

00:43:05.810 --> 00:43:07.601
the application with
the first model,

00:43:07.601 --> 00:43:10.100
and then whenever you upload
more complicated, more advanced

00:43:10.100 --> 00:43:12.630
models, they don't need
to change the application.

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:15.320
So this is actually exactly
how we built this demo.

00:43:15.320 --> 00:43:17.260
So I am more comfortable
with scikit-learn,

00:43:17.260 --> 00:43:19.640
so I started building
a scikit-learn model.

00:43:19.640 --> 00:43:21.980
And then I threw it
up on the ML Engine.

00:43:21.980 --> 00:43:23.930
And while I was doing
that, Sara was actually

00:43:23.930 --> 00:43:26.090
building a TensorFlow model.

00:43:26.090 --> 00:43:30.110
And so when I threw it up on ML
Engine, my scikit-learn model,

00:43:30.110 --> 00:43:32.990
I went off and I built the
front end that you saw.

00:43:32.990 --> 00:43:34.470
And after the
front end was done,

00:43:34.470 --> 00:43:36.080
and Sara had finished
the TensorFlow model,

00:43:36.080 --> 00:43:37.621
she just threw it
up on the ML Engine

00:43:37.621 --> 00:43:39.830
and I didn't need to
change my front end at all.

00:43:39.830 --> 00:43:42.121
I was able to immediately
just get predictions from it.

00:43:42.121 --> 00:43:45.147
I didn't need to worry about
installing dependencies.

00:43:45.147 --> 00:43:47.480
And if you've tried to embed
machine-learning algorithms

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:49.880
directly into applications,
you know how much of a pain

00:43:49.880 --> 00:43:50.810
this can be.

00:43:50.810 --> 00:43:54.140
So that's what's really
exciting about ML Engine.

00:43:54.140 --> 00:43:56.840
OK, so our model is uploaded.

00:43:56.840 --> 00:43:58.500
We'll go back to our app.

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:00.500
We can see-- OK, we're
still getting predictions

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:02.240
from version one.

00:44:02.240 --> 00:44:05.480
Sending off to Cloud ML Engine,
and it's going to come back.

00:44:05.480 --> 00:44:06.440
Cool.

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:08.330
Version two, awesome.

00:44:08.330 --> 00:44:09.130
Let's go back here.

00:44:09.130 --> 00:44:10.580
Let's try version three.

00:44:10.580 --> 00:44:13.097
It's going to fail because we
didn't call it version three.

00:44:13.097 --> 00:44:14.180
We called it version tree.

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:20.510
And then you can see, it's a
really simple decision tree,

00:44:20.510 --> 00:44:22.680
so it doesn't perform
quite as well.

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:27.470
But just to wrap up with this,
let's create our own movie.

00:44:27.470 --> 00:44:37.050
So I'll say-- let's say the
tale of a rogue engineer trying

00:44:37.050 --> 00:44:41.080
to deploy their model.

00:44:41.080 --> 00:44:42.572
Any guesses for the genre?

00:44:42.572 --> 00:44:45.170
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:45.170 --> 00:44:47.560
OK, there's a few.

00:44:47.560 --> 00:44:49.060
Let's see scikit-learn model.

00:44:49.060 --> 00:44:50.630
See what that gets.

00:44:50.630 --> 00:44:51.130
Comedy.

00:44:51.130 --> 00:44:52.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:52.310 --> 00:44:55.200
I guess it's a funny one.

00:44:55.200 --> 00:44:57.230
Let's see TensorFlow.

00:44:57.230 --> 00:44:59.920
Action, science fiction,
that's pretty good.

00:44:59.920 --> 00:45:01.600
OK, let's try to influence this.

00:45:01.600 --> 00:45:04.282
So imagine we're a
production company.

00:45:04.282 --> 00:45:05.740
We actually want
to make a romance.

00:45:05.740 --> 00:45:06.880
So how would we call it?

00:45:06.880 --> 00:45:08.460
We would say, all
that blah, blah,

00:45:08.460 --> 00:45:10.960
blah, blah, who falls in love.

00:45:10.960 --> 00:45:13.346
Who falls in love.

00:45:13.346 --> 00:45:16.560
Let's see if any of our
models pick up on it.

00:45:16.560 --> 00:45:17.830
OK, that one doesn't.

00:45:17.830 --> 00:45:22.110
Scikit-learn, hey, we
now have a romance.

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:23.010
OK.

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:25.290
So we can switch
back to the slides.

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:27.870
I hope that's shown
you some of the power

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:31.080
that ML Engine can bring when
you're developing your custom

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:32.240
machine learning models.

00:45:32.240 --> 00:45:34.350
And especially how it can
sort of bring together

00:45:34.350 --> 00:45:36.420
your data scientists,
machine engineers,

00:45:36.420 --> 00:45:40.050
with your application
engineers in a nice harmony.

00:45:40.050 --> 00:45:42.120
With that, I will hand
you back over to Sara,

00:45:42.120 --> 00:45:43.888
and enjoy the rest
of the conference.

00:45:43.888 --> 00:45:46.278
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:50.314 --> 00:45:51.480
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks, Zach.

00:45:51.480 --> 00:45:53.465
It was an awesome demo.

00:45:53.465 --> 00:45:55.105
Back to the slides.

00:45:55.105 --> 00:45:57.230
So we covered a ton of
things in this presentation.

00:45:57.230 --> 00:46:00.130
So I just wanted to give you
a quick summary to wrap up.

00:46:00.130 --> 00:46:01.640
So if you remember
only three things

00:46:01.640 --> 00:46:05.930
from the presentation,
1, use a pre-trained API

00:46:05.930 --> 00:46:07.880
to accomplish common
machine learning tasks

00:46:07.880 --> 00:46:10.010
like image analysis,
natural language

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:11.300
processing, or translation.

00:46:11.300 --> 00:46:13.220
You don't need any of your
own training data to do that.

00:46:13.220 --> 00:46:14.600
You can get started right away.

00:46:14.600 --> 00:46:17.830
Just one REST API request.

00:46:17.830 --> 00:46:19.780
Number 2, if you want
to customize these APIs

00:46:19.780 --> 00:46:21.520
to your own specific
training data,

00:46:21.520 --> 00:46:25.130
you can use Auto ML, currently
available for images, text,

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:27.200
and translation.

00:46:27.200 --> 00:46:30.680
And then finally, as Zach just
showed us, for custom tasks,

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:33.100
you can build a model,
train it on your own data,

00:46:33.100 --> 00:46:38.040
and train and serve it on
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:46:38.040 --> 00:46:39.110
So that's all we've got.

00:46:39.110 --> 00:46:41.210
Here are some resources
if you want to learn more.

00:46:41.210 --> 00:46:43.830
I'll let folks take a photo
of that side for a moment.

00:46:50.590 --> 00:46:51.400
And thank you.

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:52.608
Thanks, everyone, for coming.

00:46:52.608 --> 00:46:54.100
[APPLAUSE]

